1. WHAT IS MENU MAGICIAN?
-------------------------

Menu Magician is an online menu tree editor.  With it you can restructure
your menu tree without taking the BBS down: edit any page's settings and
menu selections, create, rename, retype and delete pages, view the whole
tree, and search it.  Almost everything the offline Menu Tree editor does,
Menu Magician does while the board runs.

Changes take effect as soon as you save a page.  That is the point of the
module, and also its danger: see section 4 before you start.


2. INSTALLING
-------------

  1. With the BBS down, copy HVSMM.DLL, HVSMM.MDF, HVSMM.MSG and HVSMM.RLN
     into your BBS directory.
  2. Run the Menu Tree editor and add Menu Magician to your menu.  Put the
     SYSOP key (or another key only trusted sysops have) on BOTH the menu
     selection and the module page.  Anyone who can use Menu Magician can
     damage your menu tree.  A good choice is an orphan page that you
     reach with /GO.
  3. Start the BBS.  Menu Magician creates HVSMMSET.DAT, its settings
     file, on the first start.

Upgrading from Menu Magician 1.03a or earlier (Worldgroup):

  - With the BBS down, replace HVSMM.DLL and HVSMM.MSG, and copy in HVSMM.MDF
    and HVSMM.RLN.  The BBS rebuilds HVSMM.MCV from HVSMM.MSG itself.
  - No activation code is needed any more.
  - The four configuration options (GLOKEY, GLOCMD, ORPHCMD, VIEWCMD) are
    no longer in the configuration editor.  They start at their usual
    defaults (SYSOP, EDIT, !NEW, !VIEW).  If you had changed them, set
    them again online with !SETUP (section 5).
  - The module name is now "Menu Magician" (it was "Menu Magician/WG").
    The menu page that runs Menu Magician still names the old module, so
    relink it: type EDIT on the menu page that leads to it, J to that
    module page, M, and pick Menu Magician from the list (or use the
    offline Menu Tree editor).
  - Worldgroup's client/server menu tree is no longer edited: The Major
    BBS v10 has terminal mode only.  Any client/server pages in the menu
    file are left as they are.


3. GETTING STARTED
------------------

There are two ways in:

  - Select Menu Magician from the menu.  You are asked which page to
    edit.  Type a page name such as TOP.
  - Type EDIT at any menu prompt (you need the SYSOP key).  Menu Magician
    opens the page you are on.  "EDIT GAMES" opens the GAMES page.  The
    command and its key are settings (section 5).

Only one person can use Menu Magician at a time.  Anyone else is told it
is in use.

At the "Edit which page?" prompt you can also type:

  !NEW       create a new orphan page (a page with no parent menu; users
             reach it with /GO)
  !VIEW      show the whole menu tree, each page type in its own color.
             Press Enter to stop it.
  !SEARCH    list every page that mentions some text
  !SETUP     change Menu Magician's own settings (sysops only)
  ?          help
  X          leave Menu Magician

Prompts accept several answers at once, so "J GAMES" jumps straight to the
GAMES page.


4. A WORD OF WARNING
--------------------

Menu Magician changes the live menu tree.  If you delete or retype a page
that someone is using, or that a user will return to, the menu system can
be confused and, in the worst case, the BBS can stop.  Menu Magician warns
you before every risky change, and warns more strongly when other users
are online.  If you are not sure, answer No, and make the change offline
or while nobody else is on.

Some changes are refused outright: you cannot delete, rename or retype the
TOP page, or the page you entered Menu Magician from.

Changes to a page are not written until you save it with S.  If you leave
a page with unsaved changes you are asked whether to save them.  If you
hang up while editing, unsaved changes are lost.


5. SETTINGS (!SETUP)
--------------------

Type !SETUP at the "Edit which page?" prompt.  You need the SYSOP key.
Changes apply immediately, are kept when the BBS restarts, and are written
to the Audit Trail.  R resets every setting to its default.

  1. Key for the global edit command      default SYSOP
     The key a user needs to use the global edit command.

  2. Global edit command                  default EDIT
     Typed at a menu prompt, opens Menu Magician on the current page.

  3. Create-orphan-page command           default !NEW
  4. View-menu-tree command               default !VIEW
     Commands at the "Edit which page?" prompt.  Change them if they clash
     with a global command from another module.

The settings are stored in HVSMMSET.DAT.  There are no options for them in
the configuration editor.


6. EDITING A PAGE
-----------------

Every page shows three common fields:

  D  Disabled          a disabled page cannot be used until it is enabled
  G  Allow /GO         whether users may /GO to this page
  K  Key for /GO       the key needed to /GO here (X clears it)

and these commands:

  S  Save the page                 !  Delete the page (*)
  J  Jump to another page          T  Change the page's type (*)
  P  Jump to the parent page       R  Rename the page (*)
  X  Stop editing this page        ?  Help
                                   (*) risky: you are warned first

File pages:

  F  Display file        the file shown to the user
  H  Display page header

Module pages:

  M  Module name         the module entered.  "?" lists the installed
                         modules; pick one by number or type its name.
                         A module that is not loaded yet loads at the next
                         BBS restart.
  C  Command string      passed to the module as if the user typed it
                         (global commands are allowed here)
  H  Display page header

Menu pages:

  A  Auto-select         route users to a selection by their keys
  N  Default display     the BBS builds the menu from the selections
  F  Display file(s)     base name of the menu file, when not default
  L  Short title         the menu's title (the TITLE text variable)
  M  List selections by description
  C  List selections by destination page
  1-25                   edit that menu selection

On a menu page, J followed by one letter jumps through that selection, so
"JT" goes from TOP to the page behind its T selection.


7. EDITING A MENU SELECTION
---------------------------

Type a selection's number (1-25) on its menu page.

  C  Selection character   the key users press.  It must be unique on
                           the page.
  H  Short description     the text in the default menu
  A  Key required          users without it cannot use (or see) it
  D  Display if no access  Hide, Dim or Show
  P  Destination page      where it leads.  A new name creates the page
                           (you are asked its type).  An orphan page's
                           name adopts the orphan.  The page it led to
                           before becomes an orphan.
  W  Swap with another selection
  S  Save the page
  X  Back to the menu page

A selection with no destination page is removed when you leave it.

When you restrict a selection with a key, put the same key on the
destination page's "Key for /GO", or users can reach it with /GO anyway.


8. FILES
--------

  HVSMM.DLL      the module
  HVSMM.MDF      module description
  HVSMM.MSG      all text, editable in the configuration editor.  Changing
                 values appear as named text variables such as HVSMM_PAGE,
                 so you can reword messages freely.
  HVSMM.RLN      this manual
  HVSMMSET.DAT   settings (created on first start)

Menu Magician edits the BBS's own menu file, WGSMENU2.DAT.  Back it up
before large changes.


9. VERSION HISTORY
------------------

  1.4.0  First release for The Major BBS v10.  No activation code.  The
         settings moved from the configuration editor to the online !SETUP
         screen.  Text uses named text variables.  Terminal mode only.
         Module name "Menu Magician".  See CHANGELOG.MD for details.
  1.03a  Last High Velocity Software release, for Worldgroup.
